#!/usr/bin/env zsh
# pj -- the light project launcher for Claude Code (F3, W-20260921-13).
#
# WHY A SCRIPT AND NOT AN ALIAS. Until 2026-09-21 `pj` was an alias in ~/.zshrc. An
# alias is read ONCE, when the shell starts, so a shell opened before the alias changed
# kept launching the OLD form for as long as it lived. Herdr keeps panes alive for days,
# and four of the last seven pj sessions ran without pj-global/RULES.md that way (P5.7,
# P6a). A script is read from disk at every launch: it cannot go stale.
#
# What it does, and only that (the same steps the alias + __claude_launch did):
#   1. builds the claude argv: project-only settings, the ONE combined system-prompt file
#      pj-prompt-file prints, the plugin dirs (four since AgentRelay was parked 2026-09-24,
#      D-20260924-A07), skip-permissions, effort high;
#   2. sources ~/.zsh_claude_launch (the shared launch function, also used by the `c`
#      family from .zshrc) and hands the argv to __claude_launch, which prints the last
#      commit's files to the tty, reads the two keychain entries, refreshes the GH
#      rate-limit cache, starts the per-session ssh-agent, sets the env vars and runs
#      claude. Anything after `pj` on the command line is appended (`pj -p x`,
#      `pj --resume`, `pj --model haiku` all pass through untouched).
#
# Own flags, recognised ONLY as the leading arguments so pass-through stays pure:
#   pj --profile <name>      launch under that profile (F5a). Must come FIRST, before
#                            --dry-run. PJ_PROFILE=<name> does the same without a flag.
#   pj --dry-run [args...]   print the exact claude argv, one entry per line, exit 0. No
#                            launch, no keychain, no ssh-agent. pj-launch-check and
#                            pj-health read it.
#   pj --selftest            prove the dry-run arms (below). Exit 0 all green, 1 otherwise.
#
# PROFILES (F5a, ruled by Gavin 2026-09-21). A profile is a key:value file under
# ~/.config/pj/profiles/, stowed from home/.config/pj/profiles/. It names the config dir,
# the settings file, the prompt source, the plugin dirs, the always-appended flags, the
# mode and whether the start card prints. `default` reproduces this script's built-in
# values exactly, so a machine with NO profiles dir stowed behaves as it always did --
# every value below has the old hardcoded constant as its fallback, and a missing or
# unreadable profile file is a silent no-op rather than a refusal.
#
# THE MERGE RULE: the DEFAULT profile's `required` flags are appended to every profile,
# whatever that profile says, so no profile can drop --dangerously-skip-permissions or
# --effort high by forgetting the key.
#
# THREE MORE KEYS, F5b 2026-09-21, from the W-20260920-A03 audit. Gavin ruled these three
# are a per-profile choice rather than something every pj session gets:
#   model: <name>            --model <name>. Unset = the account default (Opus 5 here).
#   remote_control: on|off   remoteControlAtStartup. Unset = leave it to the org default.
#   voice: on|off            voiceEnabled (Claude Code's own voice, NOT the hook_runner
#                            audio pipeline, which was PARKED at the same gate).
# Unset means EMIT NOTHING, never "emit the default", so a profile that names none of
# them produces the same argv it did before this change.
#
# TWO MORE THINGS A NON-DEFAULT PROFILE GETS, F5c 2026-09-22 (gate Q1(a) and Q2):
#   1. a SHARED SESSION REGISTRY. On a real launch the profile's config dir is given
#      `sessions -> ~/.claude/sessions`, one symlink, so sessions in the two config dirs
#      see each other in ListAgents and can SendMessage by name BOTH ways. The long form,
#      and why a directory link rather than per-session links, is above
#      ensure_registry_link. Never created on --dry-run.
#   2. a NAME -- since F5d 2026-09-22, EVERY profile gets one, the default included, and it
#      travels in the environment (`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME`) rather than argv. The long
#      form, the measurements and the shape are above build_session_name.
# The registry link applies ONLY when config_dir differs from ~/.claude. The name applies
# everywhere and still costs argv nothing, so the default launch stays byte-identical
# (selftest arms 12, 20g, 21b).
#
# THE CONFIG DIR IS THE ONLY THING THAT ACTUALLY MOVES. Measured 2026-09-21 with pj's
# real argv under a scratch CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR: the appended prompt file, the output
# style and all five --plugin-dir dirs still loaded from ~/.claude, because every path
# pj passes is absolute. What moves with the config dir is login, folder trust,
# ~/.claude.json, transcripts and auto memory -- and nothing pj owns. `config_dir:
# ~/.claude` is therefore treated as "do not export the variable at all", so the default
# launch keeps an UNSET CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR rather than one set to its own default.
#
# THREE MORE KEYS, P10 2026-09-22, so a BRANCH of dot-claude checked out as a sibling
# config dir can be the thing a session actually loads (the A/B this stage rests on).
# Measured before they existed: with only `config_dir` moved, the rules file and every
# plugin dir were still read from ~/.claude, so an edit on the branch changed nothing a
# session saw. Each key is EMPTY by default and empty means "the old path", so the default
# profile's argv stays byte-identical (arm 12) and pj-prompt-file's own defaults apply.
#   prompt_sources: <a:b>   colon list handed to pj-prompt-file as PJ_PROMPT_SOURCES.
#                           `~` expands per entry. Only used when prompt: is pj-prompt-file.
#   prompt_out: <path>      the cache file that build writes, as PJ_PROMPT_OUT. A branch
#                           profile MUST set its own, or it overwrites the default cache
#                           and the next default launch trips pj-launch-check.
#   plugins_root: <dir>     the directory the `plugins` entries are joined to; default
#                           ~/.claude. pj-launch-check reads the same key, so its
#                           declared-vs-passed comparison follows the root too.
#   function_hooks: on|off  Claude Mods (function hooks, early access). ON by default for
#                           every profile (D-20260923-A08): pj exports
#                           CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_FUNCTION_HOOKS=1. `off` exports nothing, which
#                           is the negative-control lane for testing a mod. Remove the key
#                           and the export once Claude Code ships mods without the flag.
#
# Refuses (exit 2, one line on stderr) when pj-prompt-file prints nothing: a launch
# without the rules file is the failure this script exists to prevent, so it never
# launches half-loaded. Also refuses when ~/.zsh_claude_launch is missing (stow it).
#
# Paths under ~ only. No secrets in this file; the keychain is read inside
# __claude_launch and never printed. Seams (the selftest sets them, nobody else should):
#   PJ_LAUNCH_FILE   the launch-function file (default ~/.zsh_claude_launch)
#   PJ_PROMPT_OUT    honoured by pj-prompt-file (its own seam), so the dry run can be
#                    pointed at a fixture cache
#   PJ_PROFILE_DIR   the profiles folder (default ~/.config/pj/profiles)
set -u
emulate -L zsh

SELF="${0:A}"   # captured at top level: inside a zsh function $0 is the FUNCTION name
LAUNCH_FILE="${PJ_LAUNCH_FILE:-$HOME/.zsh_claude_launch}"
PLUGIN_DIRS=(skills/herdr skills/ISA pj-voice pj)
PROFILE_DIR="${PJ_PROFILE_DIR:-${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/pj/profiles}"
typeset -ga PJ_ARGV   # not ARGV: in zsh that is the positional parameters
typeset -ga PJ_ENV    # VAR=value pairs the launch exports; EMPTY for the default profile

# --- the profile ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Every P_* below starts at the value this script hardcoded before profiles existed, so
# a box with no profiles dir stowed launches exactly as it always did. THAT IS THE
# FALLBACK, not an error path: `pj` must never refuse because a config file is absent.
P_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude"
P_SETTING_SOURCES="project,local"
P_SETTINGS="$HOME/.claude/settings.project.json"
P_PROMPT="pj-prompt-file"
P_PLUGINS="skills/herdr skills/ISA pj-voice pj"
P_REQUIRED="--dangerously-skip-permissions --effort high"
P_MODE="normal"
P_CARD="on"
# F5b. The three keys Gavin made per-profile at the F5b gate out of the 47 audited in
# W-20260920-A03. ALL THREE DEFAULT TO EMPTY, and empty means EMIT NOTHING -- not "emit
# the default value". That distinction is what keeps the default profile's argv
# byte-identical to the pre-F5a launcher, which is arm 1 of this selftest.
P_MODEL=""
P_REMOTE_CONTROL=""
P_VOICE=""
# P10: empty means "the path the tool already uses" (see the header). plugins_root is the
# one with a real default, because build_argv joins it unconditionally.
P_PROMPT_SOURCES=""
P_PROMPT_OUT=""
P_PLUGINS_ROOT="$HOME/.claude"
# D-20260923-A08: mods on by default until Claude Code ships them without the flag.
P_FUNCTION_HOOKS="on"
PROFILE_NAME="default"

expand_tilde() { local p="$1"; print -r -- "${p/#\~/$HOME}"; }
# expand_tilde_list <a:b:c> -- a colon list with `~` expanded in EVERY entry, not just the
# first. `${p/#\~/$HOME}` anchors at the start of the string, so a bare expand_tilde on the
# whole list would leave `a:~/b` half-expanded and pj-prompt-file would then skip `~/b` as
# missing, silently, and launch with one source. Arm 23b pins this.
expand_tilde_list() {
  local in="$1" out="" e
  local IFS=':'
  for e in ${=in}; do
    [[ -n "$e" ]] || continue
    out="${out:+$out:}${e/#\~/$HOME}"
  done
  print -r -- "$out"
}

# read_profile <file> -- apply the keys it holds; unknown keys and comments ignored.
# Silent on a missing file, by design (see the fallback note above).
read_profile() {
  # extended_glob is what makes `[[:space:]]#` mean "zero or more spaces" in the trims
  # below. `local_options` scopes it to this function, so nothing else sees it.
  setopt local_options extended_glob
  local f="$1" k v line
  [[ -r "$f" ]] || return 0
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    case "$line" in ''|'#'*) continue ;; esac
    [[ "$line" == *:* ]] || continue
    k="${line%%:*}"; v="${line#*:}"
    k="${k##[[:space:]]#}"; k="${k%%[[:space:]]#}"
    v="${v##[[:space:]]#}"; v="${v%%[[:space:]]#}"
    case "$k" in
      config_dir)      P_CONFIG_DIR="$(expand_tilde "$v")" ;;
      setting_sources) P_SETTING_SOURCES="$v" ;;
      settings)        P_SETTINGS="$(expand_tilde "$v")" ;;
      prompt)          P_PROMPT="$v" ;;
      plugins)         P_PLUGINS="$v" ;;
      required)        P_REQUIRED="$v" ;;
      mode)            P_MODE="$v" ;;
      card)            P_CARD="$v" ;;
      model)           P_MODEL="$v" ;;
      remote_control)  P_REMOTE_CONTROL="$v" ;;
      voice)           P_VOICE="$v" ;;
      prompt_sources)  P_PROMPT_SOURCES="$(expand_tilde_list "$v")" ;;
      prompt_out)      P_PROMPT_OUT="$(expand_tilde "$v")" ;;
      plugins_root)    P_PLUGINS_ROOT="$(expand_tilde "$v")" ;;
      function_hooks)  P_FUNCTION_HOOKS="$v" ;;
    esac
  done < "$f"
}

# load_profile <name> -- the default profile first (it supplies every fallback AND the
# `required` flags every profile inherits), then the named one on top.
load_profile() {
  local name="$1" req_default
  PROFILE_NAME="$name"
  read_profile "$PROFILE_DIR/default"
  req_default="$P_REQUIRED"
  if [[ "$name" != default ]]; then
    if [[ ! -r "$PROFILE_DIR/$name" ]]; then
      print -u2 -- "pj: REFUSED: no profile '$name' at ${PROFILE_DIR/#$HOME/~}/$name; pj-health lists the profiles it can see"
      return 2
    fi
    read_profile "$PROFILE_DIR/$name"
  fi
  # THE MERGE RULE (ruled at the F5a gate): the default profile's `required` flags are
  # appended to every profile. A profile that omits or shortens `required` cannot drop
  # them; a profile that repeats one does not get it twice.
  local t
  for t in ${=req_default}; do
    [[ " $P_REQUIRED " == *" $t "* ]] || P_REQUIRED="$P_REQUIRED $t"
  done
  return 0
}

# --- the session name (F5d gate Q1, ruled by Gavin 2026-09-22) -------------------------
#
# EVERY profile's session is named now, the DEFAULT one included, and the name travels in
# the ENVIRONMENT rather than in argv. `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME` sets the display name
# exactly as `-n/--name` does. Measured 2026-09-22 against Claude Code 2.1.278, three arms
# and two controls: the variable is honoured and the registry then records NO `nameSource`
# field at all, where `--name` records "user" and no name at all records "derived". That
# absence is how you tell the two apart.
#
# THAT IS WHY THE DEFAULT PROFILE CAN HAVE A NAME. F5c could not give it one, because a
# `--name` entry would have broken the byte-identical argv guarantee arm 12 holds. An
# environment variable adds nothing to argv, so the guarantee is untouched and the default
# session stops being anonymous in a shared registry.
#
# THE SHAPE: <repo-or-alias>-<role>[-<topic>]
#   repo first, so a mixed ListAgents groups by project and sorts;
#   role is `main` for the default profile and the profile's own name otherwise;
#   topic is the working directory's basename when it is not the repo root, which is
#   exactly the c2 worktree case and nothing else.
#
#   ~/CODE/.../fifty-shades-of-dotfiles, default    -> fifty-shades-of-dotfiles-main
#   the same repo, --profile scratch                -> fifty-shades-of-dotfiles-scratch
#   <repo>/.worktree/scratch/f5d, --profile scratch -> fifty-shades-of-dotfiles-scratch-f5d
#
# The repo part is the repo BASENAME as it stands. A repo that wants a shorter one
# declares `session-alias: <word>` in its own `.claude/pj-homes`; there is deliberately no
# hidden alias table anywhere, so the name is always explainable from two files.
#
# NO RANDOM SUFFIX, AND THIS REVERSES F5c. The suffix was there because this file said a
# collision "would otherwise be renamed by Claude Code and the prefix lost". MEASURED
# 2026-09-22 and that sentence is FALSE: two interactive sessions launched as `f5d-dup`
# ended up `f5d-dup` and `f5d-dup-twinkly-lake`, the second recording nameSource
# "collision", and ListAgents listed BOTH, distinctly. The prefix is kept and nothing is
# lost, so the suffix bought nothing and cost every name its readability.
#
# ONE HOLE, LEFT OPEN DELIBERATELY (gate Q5d). That collision check runs only for
# entrypoint `cli`, a real terminal launch. A headless `claude -p` is entrypoint `sdk-cli`
# and does NOT check: measured 2026-09-22, a `-p` probe took the name of a LIVE interactive
# session in the same registry and both kept it. `kind` says "interactive" for both, so
# `kind` is not the field to read -- `entrypoint` is. Probes only; no real pj or c2 session
# is affected, and closing it would put a registry read in front of every --dry-run and
# every selftest arm.
#
# THE 200-CHARACTER CAP IS OURS, NOT A GUESS. Measured by bisection: 200 characters are
# stored verbatim, 201 are silently truncated to 200 with nameSource still "user" and exit
# 0. Nothing here comes close, but a truncation you did not ask for is the kind of silence
# this repo writes up, so the cut is made here where it is visible.

# session_alias <repo root> -- the repo's own short name, or empty.
session_alias() {
  setopt local_options extended_glob
  local f="$1/.claude/pj-homes" v
  [[ -r "$f" ]] || return 0
  v="$(sed -n 's/^[[:space:]]*session-alias:[[:space:]]*//p' "$f" 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
  v="${v%%[[:space:]]#}"
  print -r -- "$v"
}

# name_repo_root -- the MAIN repo, never the worktree.
#
# `--show-toplevel` in a linked worktree returns the WORKTREE, so a c2 session would name
# itself after its topic twice and lose the repo. The common dir is always <main repo>/.git
# whichever checkout you stand in, so its parent is the main repo. Same rule c2 uses.
name_repo_root() {
  local cdir
  cdir="$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  [[ -n "$cdir" ]] || return 1
  [[ "$cdir" == /* ]] || cdir="$PWD/$cdir"
  print -r -- "${cdir:h:A}"
}

# in_linked_worktree -- rc 0 only inside a LINKED worktree, never in the main checkout.
#
# `--git-dir` is <main>/.git in the main checkout and <main>/.git/worktrees/<name> in a
# linked one, while `--git-common-dir` is <main>/.git in both. So the two differing IS the
# definition of a linked worktree, and it is the only honest test.
#
# THIS IS A CORRECTION. The first version asked `is the cwd the repo root?`, and the comment
# claimed that was "exactly the c2 worktree case and nothing else". Measured by
# /code-review: `cd ~/repo/docs && pj` produced `repo-main-docs`, so the same session got a
# different name depending on which SUBDIRECTORY it was launched from, and names became
# cwd-dependent. The claim in that comment was never tested; this one is, by arm 21j.
in_linked_worktree() {
  local gd cd_
  gd="$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  cd_="$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  [[ "$cd_" == /* ]] || cd_="$PWD/$cd_"
  [[ "${gd:A}" != "${cd_:A}" ]]
}

build_session_name() {
  local root base alias_ role topic n
  root="$(name_repo_root 2>/dev/null)" || root=""
  if [[ -n "$root" ]]; then
    alias_="$(session_alias "$root")"
    base="${alias_:-${root:t}}"
    # The topic is the WORKTREE's own name, and only a linked worktree has one. Read from
    # --show-toplevel rather than the cwd, so launching from a subdirectory OF the worktree
    # gives the same name as launching from its root -- which is the whole point of the
    # correction above, applied one level down.
    if in_linked_worktree; then
      topic="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)"
      topic="${topic:t}"
    fi
  else
    # outside a git repo there is no project to name, so the directory is the whole of it
    base="${PWD:t}"
  fi
  [[ "$PROFILE_NAME" == default ]] && role="main" || role="$PROFILE_NAME"
  n="${base}-${role}"
  [[ -n "${topic:-}" ]] && n="${n}-${topic}"
  n="${n//[^a-zA-Z0-9._-]/-}"
  print -r -- "${n[1,200]}"
}

# label_herdr_pane <name> -- put the session's name on its Herdr pane (F5d gate Q2).
#
# WHY A PANE AND NOT A WORKSPACE OR A TAB. Measured 2026-09-22 against herdr 0.8.2:
#   workspace  label already auto-tracks the live cwd, and `workspace rename` writes a
#              PERMANENT override with no way back (upstream herdr#3252, closed
#              not_planned). Renaming it would buy a string it already shows and cost the
#              tracking for ever. So pj never touches a workspace.
#   tab        one tab can hold several panes doing different things, so a launcher naming
#              it would clobber whatever else is in there. Tabs are named by the SESSION
#              that opens them, which is a convention in the herdr skill, not code here.
#   pane       one pane, one session. `pane rename` takes any characters, survives the
#              process exiting, and has `--clear`.
#
# AND THIS IS THE ONLY JOIN THERE IS. `herdr agent list` does NOT carry the session name:
# it carries `terminal_title`, and Claude Code overwrites that with the current task
# summary ("F5d: names for sessions, panes and tabs" while this was measured) and then with
# the shell prompt after exit. So a fresh idle session shows its name there and a working
# one does not -- which is exactly the property that cannot distinguish anything. The pane
# LABEL is stable, so setting it is what makes both listings read the same string.
#
# THE COST, NAMED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: the label outlives the session. A pane whose Claude
# has exited keeps the label until the next pj launch overwrites it or someone runs
# `herdr pane rename <id> --clear`. pj cannot clear it on the way out, because it `exec`s
# and leaves nobody behind to do it.
#
# Silent and harmless everywhere else: outside Herdr, with no pane id, or with no herdr
# binary it does nothing at all, and a herdr failure never blocks a launch.
label_herdr_pane() {
  local name="$1"
  [[ -n "$name" ]] || return 0
  [[ "${HERDR_ENV:-}" == 1 ]] || return 0
  [[ -n "${HERDR_PANE_ID:-}" ]] || return 0
  [[ -n "${PJ_HERDR:-herdr}" ]] || return 0
  whence -p "${PJ_HERDR:-herdr}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  "${PJ_HERDR:-herdr}" pane rename "$HERDR_PANE_ID" "$name" >/dev/null 2>&1
  return 0
}

build_argv() {
  local prompt d
  if [[ "$P_PROMPT" == pj-prompt-file ]]; then
    # P10: the two profile keys ride pj-prompt-file's OWN seams, set only when the profile
    # names them, so a profile without them leaves the tool's defaults (and a caller's
    # exported PJ_PROMPT_OUT, which the selftest relies on) exactly as they were.
    local -a penv=()
    [[ -n "$P_PROMPT_SOURCES" ]] && penv+=("PJ_PROMPT_SOURCES=$P_PROMPT_SOURCES")
    [[ -n "$P_PROMPT_OUT" ]] && penv+=("PJ_PROMPT_OUT=$P_PROMPT_OUT")
    prompt="$(env "${penv[@]}" pj-prompt-file)"
  else
    prompt="$(expand_tilde "$P_PROMPT")"
  fi
  if [[ -z "$prompt" ]]; then
    print -u2 -- "pj: REFUSED: pj-prompt-file printed no system-prompt file; run pj-prompt-file --selftest"
    return 2
  fi
  PJ_ARGV=(claude --setting-sources "$P_SETTING_SOURCES" --settings "$P_SETTINGS"
        --append-system-prompt-file "$prompt")
  if [[ "$P_PLUGINS" != none ]]; then
    for d in ${=P_PLUGINS}; do PJ_ARGV+=(--plugin-dir "$P_PLUGINS_ROOT/$d"); done
  fi
  [[ "$P_MODE" == safe ]] && PJ_ARGV+=(--safe-mode)
  # F5b: the three per-profile keys. `model` has its own flag; the other two are
  # settings keys with no flag, so they ride a SECOND --settings carrying a JSON string.
  #
  # WHY THAT WORKS, measured 2026-09-21 against Claude Code 2.1.278 with three arms:
  #   --settings <file> alone                  -> API_TIMEOUT_MS=1800000   (control)
  #   --settings <file> --settings <json>      -> F5B_OVERLAY=OVERLAYSET   (overlay applies)
  #   --settings <file> --settings <json>      -> API_TIMEOUT_MS=1800000   (file SURVIVES)
  # So the flag REPEATS and MERGES: later wins per key, earlier keys are kept. It is not
  # last-one-wins, which would have silently dropped the whole settings.project.json.
  # The third arm is the one that decided it; the first two alone cannot tell the two
  # behaviours apart.
  #
  # The overlay is emitted ONLY when a profile sets one of the two keys, so the default
  # profile passes exactly one --settings and its argv is unchanged.
  [[ -n "$P_MODEL" ]] && PJ_ARGV+=(--model "$P_MODEL")
  local ov=""
  [[ "$P_REMOTE_CONTROL" == on  ]] && ov="$ov\"remoteControlAtStartup\":true,"
  [[ "$P_REMOTE_CONTROL" == off ]] && ov="$ov\"remoteControlAtStartup\":false,"
  [[ "$P_VOICE" == on  ]] && ov="$ov\"voiceEnabled\":true,"
  [[ "$P_VOICE" == off ]] && ov="$ov\"voiceEnabled\":false,"
  [[ -n "$ov" ]] && PJ_ARGV+=(--settings "{${ov%,}}")
  # F5c's `--name` block lived here. It is GONE: the name now travels in the environment,
  # for every profile including the default one. See build_session_name above.
  PJ_ARGV+=(${=P_REQUIRED})
  # The env the launch exports. `config_dir: ~/.claude` means DO NOT EXPORT, because a
  # CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR set to its own default is not the same as one that is unset.
  PJ_ENV=()
  [[ "$P_CONFIG_DIR" != "$HOME/.claude" ]] && PJ_ENV+=("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=$P_CONFIG_DIR")
  [[ "$P_CARD" == off ]] && PJ_ENV+=("PJ_NO_CARD=1")
  # Only the literal `off` switches it off: an unknown value keeps the documented default.
  [[ "$P_FUNCTION_HOOKS" != off ]] && PJ_ENV+=("CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_FUNCTION_HOOKS=1")
  PJ_ENV+=("PJ_PROFILE=$PROFILE_NAME")
  # ALREADY SET WINS, so a stage that opens a control pane exports its own name first and
  # pj leaves it alone. Note the asymmetry: `--name` typed after `pj` beats the variable
  # (measured), so a hand-typed name still wins over both.
  #
  # AN `if` BLOCK, NOT A `&&` LIST, AND THIS COST A REAL BUG. As the LAST statement of this
  # function a bare `[[ ... ]] && ...` returns 1 when the test is false, every caller does
  # `build_argv || exit $?`, and pj then REFUSED TO LAUNCH AT ALL whenever the variable was
  # already set -- which is exactly the case this code exists to support, and which pj
  # itself creates for every session it launches. Caught by /code-review, not by arm 21f,
  # which passed in both worlds; see that arm for why.
  #
  # INHERITED IS NOT THE SAME AS CHOSEN, and telling them apart needs a second variable.
  # pj exports the name into the session it launches, so EVERYTHING that session starts --
  # a nested `pj`, a `c2 start` -- inherits it. Without this, a scratch session launched
  # from inside a pj session would take its PARENT's name and collide with it. Measured
  # 2026-09-22 immediately after the `&&` fix: `c2 start` from a named environment printed
  # "pj could not say" and would have handed the child the parent's name.
  #
  # So pj also exports PJ_SESSION_NAME, holding the same string. On the next launch:
  #   the two are EQUAL     -> the name was inherited from a parent pj. Recompute.
  #   only CLAUDE_..._NAME  -> a human or a stage chose it deliberately. Leave it.
  # A stage that wants a name simply exports CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME and not the marker,
  # which is what "export it before pj" already means.
  local want="${CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME:-}"
  if [[ -n "$want" && "$want" == "${PJ_SESSION_NAME:-}" ]]; then
    want=""   # inherited from the parent pj session, not chosen
  fi
  if [[ -z "$want" ]]; then
    want="$(build_session_name)"
    PJ_ENV+=("CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME=$want")
  fi
  PJ_ENV+=("PJ_SESSION_NAME=$want")
  return 0
}

# --- dot-claude freshness (P8a gate Q4, ruled by Gavin 2026-09-21) --------------
# FIRE AND FORGET. Nothing waits on this: a measured fetch of dot-claude took 1354, 1208 and
# 1223 ms on this machine (three warm runs, 2026-09-21), and a second and a quarter on every
# launch is a tax nobody consented to. The start card reports what the PREVIOUS fetch left in
# the refs, so the reading is at worst one launch old and says how old it is.
#
# IT NEVER PULLS, and never merges. dot-claude holds the drawer another session may be
# writing; a pull at launch is the concurrent-write hazard D-20260920-08 exists to keep out.
#
# THREE THINGS BOUND IT, because a launcher must never hang:
#   GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0   a stale credential FAILS instead of blocking on a prompt. This is
#                           the real hazard: dot-claude is on the GitHub App HTTPS path, and
#                           an expired ghs_ token is what stopped `pj-wrap push` twice today.
#   low-speed abort         a stalled transfer gives up after 8 s rather than hanging on.
#   detached, output binned  nothing it prints can reach the terminal or a pipe.
# Skipped entirely when the last fetch is under 10 minutes old (a relaunch is not new news)
# or when PJ_NO_FETCH is set.
kick_dotclaude_fetch() {
  [[ -n "${PJ_NO_FETCH:-}" ]] && return 0
  local dc="${OPEN_ITEMS_DOTCLAUDE:-$HOME/.claude}" fh m now
  [[ -d "$dc/.git" ]] || return 0
  fh="$dc/.git/FETCH_HEAD"
  if [[ -f "$fh" ]]; then
    m="$(stat -L -c %Y "$fh" 2>/dev/null || stat -L -f %m "$fh" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
    now="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
    [[ "$m" -gt 0 && "$now" -gt 0 && $(( now - m )) -lt 600 ]] && return 0
  fi
  ( GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 GIT_HTTP_LOW_SPEED_LIMIT=1000 GIT_HTTP_LOW_SPEED_TIME=8 \
      git -C "$dc" fetch --quiet origin >/dev/null 2>&1 & ) >/dev/null 2>&1
  return 0
}

# --- the mandatory-check runner (A16, W-20260923-A28, safety rule P3) ----------
# `open-items checks-run` is the ONLY place a reach: mandatory check-script runs; the start
# card only reads the cache it leaves. pj starts it here, detached, in the folder it launches
# in, the same fire-and-forget shape as the fetch above: nothing waits, nothing it prints can
# reach the terminal. The runner bounds itself (a 5 s timeout per script, nothing at all
# without a timeout binary) and is a no-op outside a git repo. PJ_NO_CHECKS skips it;
# PJ_OPEN_ITEMS names another open-items (selftest seam).
kick_checks_run() {
  [[ -n "${PJ_NO_CHECKS:-}" ]] && return 0
  local oi="${PJ_OPEN_ITEMS:-open-items}"
  whence -p "$oi" >/dev/null 2>&1 || [[ -x "$oi" ]] || return 0
  git rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  ( "$oi" checks-run </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 & ) >/dev/null 2>&1
  return 0
}

# --- the shared session registry (F5c, gate Q1 option (a), 2026-09-22) ------------
# Claude Code registers ONE `<pid>.json` plus one `<pid>.<hex>.key` per LIVE session under
# `<config_dir>/sessions/`, and builds ListAgents by reading ONLY that folder. The socket
# it names in that file -- `/tmp/cc-socks/<pid>.sock` -- is GLOBAL, shared by every config
# dir on the machine. So the TRANSPORT already spans profiles and only the ADDRESS BOOK is
# split, which is the whole of W-20260921-A37.
#
# One symlink fixes it: `<config_dir>/sessions -> ~/.claude/sessions`. Both sides then see
# each other in ListAgents and can SendMessage BY NAME in both directions, with no
# per-session machinery anywhere.
#
# WHY A DIRECTORY LINK AND NOT PER-SESSION LINKS, all measured 2026-09-22 with two live
# sessions in two config dirs:
#   - the reader does NOT follow a symlinked FILE. The identical registry pair was listed
#     as a COPY and not listed as a SYMLINK, same files, same minute, nothing else changed.
#     So the brief's per-session-link shape cannot work at all.
#   - per-session COPIES do work, and were proven both ways -- but a copy FREEZES: the live
#     session rewrites `status` and `updatedAt` in its own file and the copy keeps the old
#     values. Worse, it outlives the peer: after the session exited its copy sat there
#     naming a dead pid until Claude Code's own sweep reaped it.
#   - with a shared DIRECTORY the live session writes and removes ITS OWN file, so there is
#     nothing to go stale and nothing to sweep. Proven: the scratch session's entry appeared
#     in ~/.claude/sessions at launch and was gone from it seconds after /exit.
#
# IT NEVER TOUCHES ~/.claude/sessions. That folder is the default profile's own registry
# AND this link's target; replacing it would cut every default-profile session adrift.
#
# IT NEVER REFUSES A LAUNCH. Every failure path returns 0 after one line on stderr: a
# launcher that will not start because a convenience link could not be made is worse than
# one without the link. The only destructive call is `rmdir`, which by definition cannot
# lose data -- it fails on a non-empty directory, and that failure IS the guard.
ensure_registry_link() {
  local cfg="$1" target="${PJ_SESSIONS_DIR:-$HOME/.claude/sessions}" link cur
  [[ -n "$cfg" && "$cfg" != "$HOME/.claude" ]] || return 0
  link="$cfg/sessions"
  mkdir -p "$target" 2>/dev/null || return 0
  if [[ -L "$link" ]]; then
    cur="$(readlink "$link" 2>/dev/null)"
    [[ "$cur" == "$target" ]] && return 0
    if [[ -e "$link" ]]; then
      # points somewhere real and different: a human's choice, left alone but SAID
      print -u2 -- "pj: NOTE: ${link/#$HOME/~} points at $cur, not ~/.claude/sessions; leaving it. Cross-config-dir ListAgents will not work for profile '$PROFILE_NAME'."
      return 0
    fi
    # DANGLING: the only thing it can mean is a target that moved, so repair it. `ln -sfn`
    # replaces the link in one call rather than deleting and recreating it.
    ln -sfn "$target" "$link" 2>/dev/null || \
      print -u2 -- "pj: NOTE: could not repair the dangling link ${link/#$HOME/~}."
    return 0
  fi
  if [[ -d "$link" ]]; then
    # a REAL directory. Empty means a config dir that has simply never had the link; adopt
    # it. NON-EMPTY means a session is registered in it right now, and taking the folder
    # away would strand that session's own exit cleanup.
    if [[ -n "$(print -l -- "$link"/*(NDY1) 2>/dev/null)" ]]; then
      print -u2 -- "pj: NOTE: ${link/#$HOME/~} holds live session files, so it was NOT replaced by the shared-registry link. Close those sessions and relaunch."
      return 0
    fi
    rmdir "$link" 2>/dev/null || { print -u2 -- "pj: NOTE: could not replace ${link/#$HOME/~} with the shared-registry link."; return 0; }
  elif [[ -e "$link" ]]; then
    print -u2 -- "pj: NOTE: ${link/#$HOME/~} exists and is not a directory; leaving it."
    return 0
  else
    mkdir -p "$cfg" 2>/dev/null || return 0
  fi
  ln -s "$target" "$link" 2>/dev/null || \
    print -u2 -- "pj: NOTE: could not create ${link/#$HOME/~} -> ~/.claude/sessions; cross-config-dir ListAgents will not work for profile '$PROFILE_NAME'."
  return 0
}

selftest() {
  local pass=0 fail=0 out rc root n d
  ok()  { pass=$((pass + 1)); print -- "  ok   $1"; }
  bad() { fail=$((fail + 1)); print -- "  FAIL $1"; print -- "       got: $2"; }
  root="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/pj-selftest.XXXXXX")" || exit 1
  print -- "pj selftest in $root"
  # 1. dry-run argv carries the cache prompt file and all five plugin dirs, first entry is claude
  out="$(PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run 2>"$root/err1")"; rc=$?
  [[ $rc -eq 0 ]] && ok "1 dry-run exits 0" || bad "1 dry-run rc" "$rc $(<"$root/err1")"
  [[ "${out%%$'\n'*}" == claude ]] && ok "1 argv[0] is claude" || bad "1 argv[0]" "${out%%$'\n'*}"
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n--append-system-prompt-file\n'"$root/prompt.md"$'\n'* ]] && ok "1 carries the prompt cache path" || bad "1 prompt path" "$out"
  n=0; for d in "${PLUGIN_DIRS[@]}"; do [[ "$out" == *$'\n--plugin-dir\n'"$HOME/.claude/$d"$'\n'* ]] && n=$((n + 1)); done
  [[ $n -eq ${#PLUGIN_DIRS} ]] && ok "1 all ${#PLUGIN_DIRS} plugin dirs present" || bad "1 plugin dirs" "$n of ${#PLUGIN_DIRS}"
  # 1b. AgentRelay is PARKED (D-20260924-A07, 2026-09-24): no pj argv may load it. Its files stay.
  #     Read through THIS repo's profiles (the stowed ones may be an older master copy), for the
  #     built-in fallback, the default profile and the memory profile.
  local ppk="${SELF:h:h:h}/.config/pj/profiles" park=""
  [[ ${#PLUGIN_DIRS} -eq 4 && "${PLUGIN_DIRS[*]}" != *AgentRelay* ]] || park="$park fallback:${PLUGIN_DIRS[*]}"
  for f in default memory; do
    [[ -r "$ppk/$f" ]] || { park="$park ${f}:unreadable"; continue; }
    grep -q AgentRelay "$ppk/$f" && park="$park ${f}:lists-AgentRelay"
  done
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$ppk" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" == *AgentRelay* ]] && park="$park dry-run:loads-AgentRelay"
  [[ -z "$park" ]] && ok "1b AgentRelay is parked: not in the fallback, the default or memory profile, or the dry-run" || bad "1b AgentRelay parked" "$park"
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n--setting-sources\nproject,local\n'* ]] && ok "1 setting-sources project,local" || bad "1 setting-sources" "$out"
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n--settings\n'"$HOME/.claude/settings.project.json"$'\n'* ]] && ok "1 project settings file" || bad "1 settings" "$out"
  # 2. pass-through: -p x --model haiku land at the end, in order
  out="$(PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run -p x --model haiku 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n-p\nx\n--model\nhaiku' ]] && ok "2 -p x --model haiku appended in order" || bad "2 pass-through" "$out"
  # 3. a claude flag in first position is NOT eaten: --dry-run only counts as \$1
  out="$(PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run --resume --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n--resume\n--dry-run' ]] && ok "3 a later --dry-run passes through as a claude arg" || bad "3 later flag" "$out"
  # 4. no prompt file: refuses with rc 2, prints nothing on stdout
  out="$(PJ_PROMPT_SOURCES="$root/none-a:$root/none-b" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/p2.md" "$SELF" --dry-run 2>"$root/err4")"; rc=$?
  [[ $rc -eq 2 && -z "$out" ]] && ok "4 no prompt source -> REFUSED rc 2, no argv" || bad "4 refusal" "rc=$rc out=$out"
  grep -q REFUSED "$root/err4" && ok "4 refusal is announced on stderr" || bad "4 stderr" "$(<"$root/err4")"
  # 5. control: the launch file is found (the real launch path exists on this machine)
  [[ -r "$LAUNCH_FILE" ]] && ok "5 launch file readable: ${LAUNCH_FILE/#$HOME/~}" || bad "5 launch file" "$LAUNCH_FILE missing (stow?)"
  # 6. control: __claude_launch is defined by that file, in a clean zsh
  out="$(zsh -fc "source '$LAUNCH_FILE' && whence -w __claude_launch" 2>&1)"
  [[ "$out" == *": function" ]] && ok "6 launch file defines __claude_launch" || bad "6 function" "$out"
  # 7. negative control: a missing launch file is REFUSED before any launch (dry-run untouched)
  out="$(PJ_LAUNCH_FILE="$root/nope" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
  [[ $rc -eq 0 && "${out%%$'\n'*}" == claude ]] && ok "7 dry-run does not need the launch file" || bad "7 dry-run vs launch file" "rc=$rc"
  # 8. the dry-run path NEVER kicks a fetch: a real git repo is left with no FETCH_HEAD.
  #    The fixture is a real repo with a real (unreachable) remote, so a fetch WOULD write.
  local dc="$root/dotclaude"
  git init -q --bare "$root/origin.git" >/dev/null 2>&1
  git init -q "$dc" >/dev/null 2>&1
  git -C "$dc" remote add origin "$root/origin.git" >/dev/null 2>&1
  PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" OPEN_ITEMS_DOTCLAUDE="$dc" "$SELF" --dry-run >/dev/null 2>&1
  [[ ! -e "$dc/.git/FETCH_HEAD" ]] && ok "8 --dry-run kicks no fetch (no FETCH_HEAD written)" || bad "8 dry-run fetched" "FETCH_HEAD exists"
  # 9. the guard: PJ_NO_FETCH, a non-repo, and a fresh FETCH_HEAD each return 0 and fetch nothing
  PJ_NO_FETCH=1 OPEN_ITEMS_DOTCLAUDE="$dc" kick_dotclaude_fetch
  [[ $? -eq 0 && ! -e "$dc/.git/FETCH_HEAD" ]] && ok "9 PJ_NO_FETCH suppresses the fetch" || bad "9 PJ_NO_FETCH" "fetched or rc!=0"
  OPEN_ITEMS_DOTCLAUDE="$root/not-a-repo" kick_dotclaude_fetch
  [[ $? -eq 0 ]] && ok "9 a directory with no .git is a silent no-op" || bad "9 non-repo" "rc=$?"
  : > "$dc/.git/FETCH_HEAD"
  OPEN_ITEMS_DOTCLAUDE="$dc" kick_dotclaude_fetch
  [[ $? -eq 0 ]] && ok "9 a FETCH_HEAD under 10 min old skips the fetch" || bad "9 fresh FETCH_HEAD" "rc=$?"
  # 10. POSITIVE CONTROL for arm 8: with no guard in the way the fetch IS reached.
  #     Without this arm, arm 8 passes just as well when the function is broken and never
  #     fetches at all -- "no FETCH_HEAD" is true in both worlds.
  #     A SECOND, VIRGIN fixture, never a cleared one: `rm` here is the Trash-routed shim,
  #     which can refuse and leave the file (measured 2026-09-21 in this very arm), and then
  #     the control would pass on a stale file instead of a fresh fetch.
  local dc2="$root/dotclaude2"
  git init -q "$dc2" >/dev/null 2>&1
  git -C "$dc2" remote add origin "$root/origin.git" >/dev/null 2>&1
  [[ ! -e "$dc2/.git/FETCH_HEAD" ]] || bad "10 fixture was not virgin" "FETCH_HEAD already present"
  OPEN_ITEMS_DOTCLAUDE="$dc2" kick_dotclaude_fetch
  local waited=0
  while [[ $waited -lt 50 && ! -e "$dc2/.git/FETCH_HEAD" ]]; do sleep 0.1; waited=$((waited + 1)); done
  [[ -e "$dc2/.git/FETCH_HEAD" ]] && ok "10 control: with no guard the fetch IS reached (FETCH_HEAD appears)" \
    || bad "10 control did not fetch -- arm 8 proves nothing" "no FETCH_HEAD after ${waited}00ms"
  # 10b. the mandatory-check runner (A16, P3): a fake open-items that records its call.
  #      The dry run never starts it; PJ_NO_CHECKS stops it; the control shows it IS reached.
  local fake="$root/fake-open-items" mark="$root/checks-ran"
  print -r -- '#!/bin/sh
[ "$1" = checks-run ] && touch "'"$mark"'"' > "$fake"; chmod +x "$fake"
  PJ_OPEN_ITEMS="$fake" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run >/dev/null 2>&1
  sleep 0.3
  [[ ! -e "$mark" ]] && ok "10b --dry-run starts no checks-run" || bad "10b dry-run ran checks" "marker exists"
  ( builtin cd "$dc" && PJ_NO_CHECKS=1 PJ_OPEN_ITEMS="$fake" kick_checks_run )
  sleep 0.3
  [[ ! -e "$mark" ]] && ok "10b PJ_NO_CHECKS suppresses checks-run" || bad "10b PJ_NO_CHECKS" "marker exists"
  ( builtin cd "$root" && PJ_OPEN_ITEMS="$fake" kick_checks_run )
  sleep 0.3
  [[ ! -e "$mark" ]] && ok "10b outside a git repo checks-run is not started" || bad "10b non-repo" "marker exists"
  ( builtin cd "$dc" && PJ_OPEN_ITEMS="$fake" kick_checks_run )
  waited=0
  while [[ $waited -lt 50 && ! -e "$mark" ]]; do sleep 0.1; waited=$((waited + 1)); done
  [[ -e "$mark" ]] && ok "10b control: in a repo with no guard, checks-run IS started (detached)" \
    || bad "10b control never started checks-run -- the arms above prove nothing" "no marker after ${waited}00ms"
  # ---- profiles (F5a) -------------------------------------------------------------
  # 11. NO profiles dir at all: the argv is the built-in one. This is the consumer-box
  #     fallback, and it is arm ONE of the byte-for-byte guarantee.
  local pdir="$root/profiles" base
  base="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$root/none-such" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "${base%%$'\n'*}" == claude ]] && ok "11 no profiles dir: still launches (built-in fallback)" || bad "11 no profiles dir" "$base"
  n=0; for d in "${PLUGIN_DIRS[@]}"; do [[ "$base" == *$'\n--plugin-dir\n'"$HOME/.claude/$d"$'\n'* ]] && n=$((n + 1)); done
  [[ $n -eq ${#PLUGIN_DIRS} ]] && ok "11 fallback carries all ${#PLUGIN_DIRS} plugin dirs" || bad "11 fallback plugins" "$n of ${#PLUGIN_DIRS}"
  # 12. a `default` profile that spells out today's values produces the SAME argv, byte
  #     for byte. Hard constraint 1 of F5a, proven against arm 11's output rather than
  #     against a copy of the expected text.
  mkdir -p "$pdir"
  {
    print -- "config_dir:      ~/.claude"
    print -- "setting_sources: project,local"
    print -- "settings:        ~/.claude/settings.project.json"
    print -- "prompt:          pj-prompt-file"
    print -- "plugins:         skills/herdr skills/ISA pj-voice pj"
    print -- "required:        --dangerously-skip-permissions --effort high"
    print -- "mode:            normal"
    print -- "card:            on"
  } > "$pdir/default"
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" == "$base" ]] && ok "12 the default profile reproduces the built-in argv byte for byte" || bad "12 default profile drift" "$(diff <(print -r -- "$base") <(print -r -- "$out") | head -6)"
  # 13. the default profile exports NO CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. A variable set to its own
  #     default is not the same as an unset one, and this is the arm that keeps them apart.
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run-env 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" != *CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR* ]] && ok "13 default profile exports no CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR" || bad "13 default exported a config dir" "$out"
  [[ "$out" == *"PJ_PROFILE=default"* ]] && ok "13 default profile names itself in the env" || bad "13 PJ_PROFILE" "$out"
  # 13c. D-20260923-A08: mods are ON by default, so the default launch exports the flag.
  [[ "$out" == *"CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_FUNCTION_HOOKS=1"* ]] && ok "13c default profile exports CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_FUNCTION_HOOKS=1" || bad "13c function hooks default" "$out"
  # 14. a named profile: config dir exported, no plugin dirs, card off, safe mode absent
  {
    print -- "config_dir: $root/cfg-x"
    print -- "plugins:    none"
    print -- "card:       off"
    print -- "function_hooks: off"
  } > "$pdir/scratchy"
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile scratchy --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" != *"--plugin-dir"* ]] && ok "14 plugins: none passes no --plugin-dir" || bad "14 plugins none" "$out"
  [[ "$out" != *"--safe-mode"* ]] && ok "14 mode defaults to normal (no --safe-mode)" || bad "14 unexpected safe mode" "$out"
  # 14b. THE MERGE RULE: scratchy never says `required`, and still gets both flags.
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n--dangerously-skip-permissions\n--effort\nhigh' ]] && ok "14b required flags are inherited from the default profile" || bad "14b merge rule" "$out"
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile scratchy --dry-run-env 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" == *"CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=$root/cfg-x"* ]] && ok "14 a forked config dir IS exported" || bad "14 config dir" "$out"
  [[ "$out" == *"PJ_NO_CARD=1"* ]] && ok "14 card: off exports PJ_NO_CARD=1" || bad "14 card off" "$out"
  # 14c. function_hooks: off is the negative-control lane: no flag in the env at all.
  [[ "$out" != *FUNCTION_HOOKS* ]] && ok "14c function_hooks: off exports no flag" || bad "14c function hooks off" "$out"
  # 15. mode: safe adds --safe-mode, and ONLY then. Kept supported for P9's baseline.
  printf 'mode: safe\n' > "$pdir/safey"
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile safey --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n--safe-mode\n'* ]] && ok "15 mode: safe adds --safe-mode" || bad "15 safe mode" "$out"
  # 16. a profile that does not exist REFUSES rc 2 and prints no argv. Silence would
  #     launch the default profile under the wrong name, which is the dangerous arm.
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile nope --dry-run 2>"$root/err16")"; rc=$?
  [[ $rc -eq 2 && -z "$out" ]] && ok "16 unknown profile REFUSES rc 2, prints no argv" || bad "16 unknown profile" "rc=$rc out=$out"
  grep -q REFUSED "$root/err16" && ok "16 the refusal names itself on stderr" || bad "16 stderr" "$(<"$root/err16")"
  # 17. --profile with no name refuses; and a LATER --profile is a claude arg, not ours
  PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" "$SELF" --profile >/dev/null 2>&1; rc=$?
  [[ $rc -eq 2 ]] && ok "17 --profile with no name refuses rc 2" || bad "17 bare --profile" "rc=$rc"
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run --profile x 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n--profile\nx' ]] && ok "17 a later --profile passes through to claude" || bad "17 pass-through" "$out"
  # 18. PJ_PROFILE selects without the flag
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE=scratchy PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" != *"--plugin-dir"* ]] && ok "18 PJ_PROFILE selects the profile without a flag" || bad "18 PJ_PROFILE" "$out"
  # 19. F5b: the three per-profile keys from the W-20260920-A03 audit.
  #     19a is the POSITIVE arm and 19d the NEGATIVE one, and 19d is the arm that
  #     matters: an absent key must emit NOTHING, because emitting the default value
  #     instead would break the byte-identical guarantee arm 1 proves. A negative arm
  #     alone cannot tell "correctly silent" from "the feature never ran", so 19a-c run
  #     against the SAME fixture dir in the same breath.
  {
    print -- "model:          haiku"
    print -- "remote_control: on"
    print -- "voice:          off"
  } > "$pdir/threekeys"
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile threekeys --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n--model\nhaiku\n'* ]] && ok "19a model: emits --model <name>" || bad "19a model" "$out"
  [[ "$out" == *'"remoteControlAtStartup":true'* ]] && ok "19b remote_control: on rides a second --settings" || bad "19b remote_control" "$out"
  [[ "$out" == *'"voiceEnabled":false'* ]] && ok "19c voice: off is emitted explicitly, not omitted" || bad "19c voice" "$out"
  n=$(print -r -- "$out" | grep -c -- '^--settings$')
  [[ "$n" -eq 2 ]] && ok "19c the FILE --settings survives beside the overlay (2 of them)" || bad "19c settings count" "got $n, wanted 2"
  # 19d. the negative arm: a profile naming none of the three emits none of them.
  printf 'plugins: none\n' > "$pdir/nokeys"
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile nokeys --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" != *"--model"* ]] && ok "19d an unset model emits no --model" || bad "19d model leaked" "$out"
  [[ "$out" != *'"voiceEnabled"'* && "$out" != *'"remoteControlAtStartup"'* ]] && ok "19d unset keys emit no overlay --settings" || bad "19d overlay leaked" "$out"
  n=$(print -r -- "$out" | grep -c -- '^--settings$')
  [[ "$n" -eq 1 ]] && ok "19d exactly ONE --settings when no profile key is set" || bad "19d settings count" "got $n, wanted 1"
  # ---- 23: a branch config dir's own files are the loaded ones (P10) ----------------
  # 23a plugins_root: every --plugin-dir is joined to the root, and NONE is under ~/.claude.
  #     Both directions, because a launcher that ignored the key would pass the ~/.claude
  #     five and still "have five plugin dirs".
  local proot="$root/proot"
  printf 'plugins_root: %s\nplugins: skills/herdr skills/ISA pj-voice pj\n' "$proot" > "$pdir/rooted"
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile rooted --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  n=0; for d in "${PLUGIN_DIRS[@]}"; do [[ "$out" == *$'\n--plugin-dir\n'"$proot/$d"$'\n'* ]] && n=$((n + 1)); done
  [[ $n -eq ${#PLUGIN_DIRS} ]] && ok "23a plugins_root: all ${#PLUGIN_DIRS} plugin dirs are joined to the root" || bad "23a rooted dirs" "$n of ${#PLUGIN_DIRS} under $proot"
  #     (the --settings path is still ~/.claude/settings.project.json, by design: RECORDS
  #     does not move, so the check is on --plugin-dir pairs, not on the whole argv)
  [[ "$out" != *$'\n--plugin-dir\n'"$HOME/.claude/"* ]] && ok "23a and no --plugin-dir is under ~/.claude" || bad "23a ~/.claude plugin dir leaked" "$out"
  # 23a' `~` in plugins_root expands
  printf 'plugins_root: ~/p23-nowhere\nplugins: pj\n' > "$pdir/rootedtilde"
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile rootedtilde --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n--plugin-dir\n'"$HOME/p23-nowhere/pj"$'\n'* ]] && ok "23a' a ~ in plugins_root expands to \$HOME" || bad "23a' tilde" "$out"
  # 23b prompt_sources + prompt_out: the argv names the profile's OWN cache file, that file
  #     holds exactly the profile's sources (a + newline + b), and the DEFAULT cache the
  #     caller exported was not written by this launch. The second source is spelled with
  #     `~` so per-entry expansion is proven, not assumed.
  #     HOME is pointed at the fixture root for this one launch so `~/src-b.md` resolves
  #     there: a real file under the operator's home is not something a selftest writes.
  local sa="$root/src-a.md" sb="$root/src-b.md" pout="$root/p23/own-cache.md"
  printf 'alpha rules\n' > "$sa"; printf 'beta rules\n' > "$sb"
  printf 'prompt_sources: %s:~/src-b.md\nprompt_out: %s\nplugins: none\n' "$sa" "$pout" > "$pdir/ownprompt"
  : > "$root/default-cache.md"
  out="$(HOME="$root" PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/default-cache.md" "$SELF" --profile ownprompt --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
  [[ $rc -eq 0 && "$out" == *$'\n--append-system-prompt-file\n'"$pout"$'\n'* ]] && ok "23b prompt_out: the argv carries the profile's own cache path" || bad "23b prompt path" "rc=$rc $out"
  if cmp -s "$pout" <(cat "$sa"; printf '\n'; cat "$sb"); then ok "23b prompt_sources: the cache holds a + newline + b (both sources, ~ expanded per entry)"; else bad "23b cache bytes" "$(cat "$pout" 2>&1 | tr '\n' '|')"; fi
  [[ ! -s "$root/default-cache.md" ]] && ok "23b the caller's default cache was NOT written (still empty)" || bad "23b default cache written" "$(wc -c <"$root/default-cache.md") bytes"
  # 23c CONTROL: the default profile still runs pj-prompt-file with NO overrides, so its
  #     argv names the exported PJ_PROMPT_OUT (the same fact arm 1 rests on, restated here
  #     because 23b would pass just as well if the overrides leaked into every profile).
  out="$(PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run 2>/dev/null)"
  [[ "$out" == *$'\n--append-system-prompt-file\n'"$root/prompt.md"$'\n'* ]] && ok "23c control: the default profile still uses the caller's PJ_PROMPT_OUT" || bad "23c default prompt" "$out"
  [[ "$out" == "$base" ]] && ok "23c control: and its argv is still byte-identical to the built-in one" || bad "23c drift" "$(diff <(print -r -- "$base") <(print -r -- "$out") | head -6)"
  # ---- the shared session registry (F5c) ------------------------------------------
  # Every arm runs against a FIXTURE target via PJ_SESSIONS_DIR. Pointing these at the
  # real ~/.claude/sessions would have the suite rearranging live sessions' address book.
  local reg="$root/registry" cfgx err20
  mkdir -p "$reg/default-sessions"
  export PJ_SESSIONS_DIR="$reg/default-sessions"
  # 20a POSITIVE: a config dir with no sessions entry at all gets the link.
  cfgx="$reg/cfg-a"; mkdir -p "$cfgx"
  ensure_registry_link "$cfgx" 2>/dev/null
  [[ -L "$cfgx/sessions" && "$(readlink "$cfgx/sessions")" == "$PJ_SESSIONS_DIR" ]] \
    && ok "20a a fresh config dir gets sessions -> the default registry" || bad "20a no link" "$(ls -la "$cfgx" 2>&1 | tail -2)"
  # 20b idempotent: a second call changes nothing and says nothing.
  err20="$(ensure_registry_link "$cfgx" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
  [[ -L "$cfgx/sessions" && -z "$err20" ]] && ok "20b a second call is a silent no-op" || bad "20b not idempotent" "$err20"
  # 20c DANGLING link -> repaired. This is the arm the brief asked for by name.
  cfgx="$reg/cfg-c"; mkdir -p "$cfgx"; ln -s "$reg/no-such-target" "$cfgx/sessions"
  [[ ! -e "$cfgx/sessions" && -L "$cfgx/sessions" ]] || bad "20c fixture" "the fixture link is not dangling, so the arm proves nothing"
  ensure_registry_link "$cfgx" 2>/dev/null
  [[ "$(readlink "$cfgx/sessions")" == "$PJ_SESSIONS_DIR" ]] \
    && ok "20c a DANGLING sessions link is repaired" || bad "20c dangling" "$(readlink "$cfgx/sessions")"
  # 20d NEGATIVE: a link to a real, DIFFERENT directory is left alone and announced.
  #     Without this arm 20c passes just as well on code that clobbers every symlink it
  #     meets -- "the link now points at the target" is true in both worlds.
  cfgx="$reg/cfg-d"; mkdir -p "$cfgx" "$reg/somewhere-else"; ln -s "$reg/somewhere-else" "$cfgx/sessions"
  err20="$(ensure_registry_link "$cfgx" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
  [[ "$(readlink "$cfgx/sessions")" == "$reg/somewhere-else" ]] \
    && ok "20d a link to a real OTHER directory is NOT clobbered" || bad "20d clobbered" "$(readlink "$cfgx/sessions")"
  [[ "$err20" == *"points at"* ]] && ok "20d and it says so on stderr" || bad "20d silent" "$err20"
  # 20e a real EMPTY directory is adopted (rmdir cannot lose data; it fails if it would).
  cfgx="$reg/cfg-e"; mkdir -p "$cfgx/sessions"
  ensure_registry_link "$cfgx" 2>/dev/null
  [[ -L "$cfgx/sessions" && "$(readlink "$cfgx/sessions")" == "$PJ_SESSIONS_DIR" ]] \
    && ok "20e an EMPTY real sessions dir is replaced by the link" || bad "20e empty dir" "$(ls -la "$cfgx" 2>&1 | tail -2)"
  # 20f NON-EMPTY (a peer registered there right now) is left alone and announced. Losing
  #     this folder would strand that session's own exit cleanup.
  cfgx="$reg/cfg-f"; mkdir -p "$cfgx/sessions"; : > "$cfgx/sessions/12345.json"
  err20="$(ensure_registry_link "$cfgx" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
  [[ -d "$cfgx/sessions" && ! -L "$cfgx/sessions" && -e "$cfgx/sessions/12345.json" ]] \
    && ok "20f a NON-EMPTY sessions dir is left alone, live files intact" || bad "20f clobbered a live registry" "$(ls -la "$cfgx/sessions" 2>&1 | tail -3)"
  [[ "$err20" == *"live session files"* ]] && ok "20f and it says so on stderr" || bad "20f silent" "$err20"
  # 20g the DEFAULT config dir is never linked -- to itself or to anything else.
  err20="$(ensure_registry_link "$HOME/.claude" 2>&1 >/dev/null)"
  [[ -z "$err20" ]] && [[ ! -L "$HOME/.claude/sessions" ]] \
    && ok "20g config_dir ~/.claude is a silent no-op (the default profile is untouched)" || bad "20g touched the default" "$err20"
  ensure_registry_link "" 2>/dev/null
  [[ $? -eq 0 ]] && ok "20g an empty config_dir is a silent no-op" || bad "20g empty arg" "rc=$?"
  # 20h a config dir that does not exist yet is created along with the link.
  cfgx="$reg/cfg-h-missing"
  [[ ! -e "$cfgx" ]] || bad "20h fixture" "the missing-dir fixture already exists"
  ensure_registry_link "$cfgx" 2>/dev/null
  [[ -L "$cfgx/sessions" ]] && ok "20h a config dir that does not exist yet is created with the link" || bad "20h missing dir" "$(ls -la "$cfgx" 2>&1)"
  # 20i CONTROL: the fixture target really is a different path from the real registry, so
  #     none of 20a-20h can have been measuring ~/.claude/sessions by accident.
  [[ "$PJ_SESSIONS_DIR" != "$HOME/.claude/sessions" ]] \
    && ok "20i control: the arms ran against a fixture, not the live registry" || bad "20i control" "the suite pointed at the real registry"
  unset PJ_SESSIONS_DIR
  # 20j --dry-run creates NO link, the same contract arm 8 holds the fetch to.
  cfgx="$reg/cfg-dry"
  printf 'config_dir: %s\nplugins: none\n' "$cfgx" > "$pdir/dryreg"
  PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile dryreg --dry-run >/dev/null 2>&1
  [[ ! -e "$cfgx/sessions" ]] && ok "20j --dry-run creates no registry link" || bad "20j dry-run wrote" "$cfgx/sessions exists"
  # ---- 21: the session name (F5d gate Q1) -------------------------------------------
  #
  # Every arm runs inside a REAL throwaway git repo, because the builder reads
  # `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` and a fixture that is not a repo would exercise only
  # the fallback. Arm 21i is the control that proves the builder reads the repo it is
  # standing in rather than printing a constant.
  local nrepo nwt
  nrepo="$root/namerepo"; mkdir -p "$nrepo"
  git -C "$nrepo" init -q 2>/dev/null
  git -C "$nrepo" commit -q --allow-empty -m init 2>/dev/null
  nameof() {  # <dir> [profile args...] -> the CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME the env would carry
    local d="$1"; shift
    ( builtin cd "$d" && PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" \
        "$SELF" "$@" --dry-run-env 2>/dev/null ) | sed -n 's/^CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME=//p'
  }
  # 21a the default profile IS named now, <repo>-main
  out="$(nameof "$nrepo")"
  [[ "$out" == "namerepo-main" ]] && ok "21a the default profile is named <repo>-main" || bad "21a default name" "$out"
  # 21b THE NEGATIVE ARM, and the one that protects arm 12: the name costs argv NOTHING.
  #     A --name entry here would break the byte-identical guarantee.
  out="$( builtin cd "$nrepo" && PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run 2>/dev/null )"
  [[ "$out" != *"--name"* ]] && ok "21b no profile emits --name into argv" || bad "21b argv named" "$out"
  # 21c a non-default profile takes its own name as the role
  out="$(nameof "$nrepo" --profile scratchy)"
  [[ "$out" == "namerepo-scratchy" ]] && ok "21c a non-default profile is <repo>-<profile>" || bad "21c profile name" "$out"
  # 21d a LINKED WORKTREE adds the topic, and still names the MAIN repo. This is the arm
  #     that would fail if the builder used --show-toplevel: it would print worktopic-*.
  nwt="$nrepo/.worktree/scratch/worktopic"
  git -C "$nrepo" worktree add -q -b scratch/worktopic "$nwt" 2>/dev/null
  if [[ -d "$nwt" ]]; then
    out="$(nameof "$nwt" --profile scratchy)"
    [[ "$out" == "namerepo-scratchy-worktopic" ]] && ok "21d a worktree is <repo>-<profile>-<topic>, repo NOT the worktree" || bad "21d worktree name" "$out"
  else
    bad "21d worktree name" "could not create the fixture worktree"
  fi
  # 21e session-alias: in .claude/pj-homes replaces the basename
  mkdir -p "$nrepo/.claude"
  printf 'records: private\nsession-alias: nr\n' > "$nrepo/.claude/pj-homes"
  out="$(nameof "$nrepo")"
  [[ "$out" == "nr-main" ]] && ok "21e session-alias: replaces the repo basename" || bad "21e alias" "$out"
  # 21f ALREADY SET WINS: an exported name is left exactly alone, alias and all.
  #
  #     THREE ASSERTIONS, NOT ONE, AND THAT IS THE LESSON. The first version checked only
  #     that no CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME line came back -- which is equally true when pj
  #     correctly omits it AND when pj ABORTS printing nothing. pj was aborting (rc 1, no
  #     output at all) for exactly this input, the arm passed, and /code-review found the
  #     bug instead. The repo's own all-cases-identical rule, in the arm written to guard
  #     the feature it hid. So: the exit code, the ABSENCE, and a POSITIVE line that proves
  #     the run produced real output.
  out="$( builtin cd "$nrepo" && CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME=hand-picked PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" \
          PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run-env 2>/dev/null )"; rc=$?
  [[ $rc -eq 0 ]] && ok "21f pj still exits 0 when the name is already set" || bad "21f rc" "rc=$rc out=$out"
  [[ "$out" != *"CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME="* ]] && ok "21f an already-set name is not overwritten (pj adds nothing)" || bad "21f overwrote" "$out"
  [[ "$out" == *"PJ_PROFILE=default"* ]] && ok "21f POSITIVE: the run still produced its other env, so the absence above is a choice not a crash" || bad "21f no output" "$out"
  # And the argv path must survive it too, since that is what a real launch uses.
  out="$( builtin cd "$nrepo" && CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME=hand-picked PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" \
          PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --dry-run 2>/dev/null )"; rc=$?
  [[ $rc -eq 0 && "$out" == claude* ]] && ok "21f the argv path also survives an already-set name" || bad "21f argv" "rc=$rc out=${out:0:80}"
  # 21g' INHERITED IS NOT CHOSEN. pj exports the name into the session it launches, so a
  #      nested pj or a c2 start inherits it; without a marker the child would silently take
  #      its PARENT's name and collide with it. PJ_SESSION_NAME carries the same string, and
  #      the two being EQUAL is what identifies an inherited name.
  out="$( builtin cd "$nrepo" && CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME=namerepo-main PJ_SESSION_NAME=namerepo-main \
          PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile scratchy --dry-run-env 2>/dev/null )"; rc=$?
  [[ $rc -eq 0 ]] && ok "21g' an inherited name still launches, rc 0" || bad "21g' rc" "rc=$rc"
  #      (the session-alias from 21e is still in force here, so the expected base is `nr`)
  [[ "$out" == *"CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME=nr-scratchy"* ]] && ok "21g' an INHERITED name is RECOMPUTED for the new profile, not carried over" || bad "21g' inherited carried" "$out"
  # 21g'' THE CONTROL: the same name WITHOUT the marker is a deliberate choice and is kept.
  #       Both arms set CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME to the same value; only the marker differs.
  out="$( builtin cd "$nrepo" && CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME=namerepo-main \
          PJ_PROFILE_DIR="$pdir" PJ_PROMPT_OUT="$root/prompt.md" "$SELF" --profile scratchy --dry-run-env 2>/dev/null )"
  [[ "$out" != *"CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME="* ]] && ok "21g'' control: without the marker the same name is left alone (so 21g' measured the marker)" || bad "21g'' overwrote a chosen name" "$out"
  [[ "$out" == *"PJ_SESSION_NAME=namerepo-main"* ]] && ok "21g'' and the marker is set to it, so the NEXT nested launch recomputes" || bad "21g'' no marker" "$out"
  # OVERWRITE, do not remove. `rm` here is the Trash shim and can REFUSE inside the sandbox,
  # leaving the file exactly where it was -- and arms 21j and 21k then read the stale alias
  # as their answer. That happened, to this very line, on the run that added those arms. A
  # write always takes; a delete does not. (Same trap as arm 22's recorder files above.)
  printf 'records: private\n' > "$nrepo/.claude/pj-homes"
  out="$(nameof "$nrepo")"
  [[ "$out" == "namerepo-main" ]] && ok "21f' control: with session-alias gone the basename is back (so 21e measured the alias, not a constant)" || bad "21f' alias stuck" "$out"
  # 21g OUTSIDE a git repo the builder falls back to the directory, and does not crash
  mkdir -p "$root/notarepo"
  out="$(nameof "$root/notarepo")"
  [[ "$out" == "notarepo-main" ]] && ok "21g outside a repo the directory is the whole name" || bad "21g no repo" "$out"
  # 21h the 200-character cut is OURS and is made here, where it is visible. Claude Code
  #     truncates silently at 200 (measured: 200 verbatim, 201 -> 200, exit 0, no warning).
  local longdir="$root/$(printf 'q%.0s' {1..230})"
  mkdir -p "$longdir" 2>/dev/null
  if [[ -d "$longdir" ]]; then
    out="$(nameof "$longdir")"
    [[ ${#out} -eq 200 ]] && ok "21h a name longer than 200 is cut to 200 by pj, not silently by Claude Code" || bad "21h cut" "len=${#out}"
  else
    bad "21h cut" "could not create the long fixture dir"
  fi
  # 21i THE CONTROL for every arm above: a DIFFERENT repo must produce a DIFFERENT name.
  #     Without it, a builder that printed one constant would pass 21a and 21c alike.
  local nrepo2="$root/othername"; mkdir -p "$nrepo2"
  git -C "$nrepo2" init -q 2>/dev/null
  out="$(nameof "$nrepo2")"
  [[ "$out" == "othername-main" && "$out" != "namerepo-main" ]] && ok "21i control: a different repo gets a different name" || bad "21i control" "$out"
  # 21j A PLAIN SUBDIRECTORY IS NOT A TOPIC, and arm 21d is the control that says the topic
  #     mechanism still works. The first version of the builder added a topic for any cwd
  #     that was not the repo root, so `cd repo/docs && pj` named the session `repo-main-docs`
  #     and the same session got a different name per subdirectory. Measured by /code-review.
  mkdir -p "$nrepo/docs/deep"
  out="$(nameof "$nrepo/docs")"
  [[ "$out" == "namerepo-main" ]] && ok "21j a subdirectory of the repo adds NO topic" || bad "21j subdir topic" "$out"
  out="$(nameof "$nrepo/docs/deep")"
  [[ "$out" == "namerepo-main" ]] && ok "21j and neither does a deeper one" || bad "21j deep subdir" "$out"
  # 21k and a subdirectory OF A WORKTREE still names the worktree, not the subdirectory
  if [[ -d "$nwt" ]]; then
    mkdir -p "$nwt/sub"
    out="$(nameof "$nwt/sub" --profile scratchy)"
    [[ "$out" == "namerepo-scratchy-worktopic" ]] && ok "21k inside a worktree subdir the topic is still the WORKTREE" || bad "21k worktree subdir" "$out"
  fi

  # ---- 22: the Herdr pane label (F5d gate Q2) ---------------------------------------
  # A fake `herdr` on PATH records every call, so "it did nothing" and "it was never asked"
  # are two different files rather than the same silence.
  # EVERY ARM GETS ITS OWN RECORDER FILE, via HERDR_CALLS in the fake's environment.
  # The first version reused one path and cleared it with `rm -f` between arms. Inside the
  # Claude sandbox `rm` is the Trash shim and CAN REFUSE (trash exit 5, file left in place),
  # so the stale recording from an earlier arm was read by a later one as a finding: measured
  # by /code-review as `22f ... got: rc=0` with the rc itself correct. CLAUDE.md writes this
  # up for decided-selftest; it is the same trap. A fresh filename cannot go stale.
  local fb="$root/fakebin"; mkdir -p "$fb"
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nprintf "%%s\\n" "$*" >> "${HERDR_CALLS:-/dev/null}"\n' > "$fb/herdr"
  chmod +x "$fb/herdr"
  # 22a outside Herdr: nothing is called
  ( PATH="$fb:$PATH"; HERDR_CALLS="$root/calls.22a"; export HERDR_CALLS; unset HERDR_ENV; HERDR_PANE_ID=w1:p1 label_herdr_pane "x-main" ) >/dev/null 2>&1
  [[ ! -e "$root/calls.22a" ]] && ok "22a no HERDR_ENV -> herdr is never called" || bad "22a called" "$(<$root/calls.22a)"
  # 22b inside Herdr but with no pane id: still nothing
  ( PATH="$fb:$PATH"; HERDR_CALLS="$root/calls.22b"; export HERDR_CALLS; HERDR_ENV=1 HERDR_PANE_ID="" label_herdr_pane "x-main" ) >/dev/null 2>&1
  [[ ! -e "$root/calls.22b" ]] && ok "22b no pane id -> herdr is never called" || bad "22b called" "$(<$root/calls.22b)"
  # 22c an empty name: nothing, rather than a blank label. herdr has no guard against a
  #     blank one and a blank workspace label is unclearable, so refuse it here.
  ( PATH="$fb:$PATH"; HERDR_CALLS="$root/calls.22c"; export HERDR_CALLS; HERDR_ENV=1 HERDR_PANE_ID=w1:p1 label_herdr_pane "" ) >/dev/null 2>&1
  [[ ! -e "$root/calls.22c" ]] && ok "22c an empty name -> herdr is never called" || bad "22c called" "$(<$root/calls.22c)"
  # 22d THE POSITIVE ARM: pane id + name -> exactly one rename, with that pane and that name
  ( PATH="$fb:$PATH"; HERDR_CALLS="$root/calls.22d"; export HERDR_CALLS; HERDR_ENV=1 HERDR_PANE_ID=w1:p1 label_herdr_pane "namerepo-main" ) >/dev/null 2>&1
  if [[ -e "$root/calls.22d" ]]; then
    out="$(<"$root/calls.22d")"
    [[ "$out" == "pane rename w1:p1 namerepo-main" ]] && ok "22d a labelled pane gets exactly: pane rename <id> <name>" || bad "22d call" "$out"
    [[ "$(grep -c . "$root/calls.22d")" == 1 ]] && ok "22d exactly ONE call, not one per arm above" || bad "22d call count" "$out"
  else
    bad "22d call" "nothing was recorded"
  fi
  # 22e CONTROL for 22a-22c: the fake herdr really does record when it IS run, so those
  #     three absent files mean "not called" rather than "the recorder is broken".
  ( PATH="$fb:$PATH"; HERDR_CALLS="$root/calls.22e"; export HERDR_CALLS; herdr control arm ) >/dev/null 2>&1
  [[ -e "$root/calls.22e" ]] && ok "22e control: the fake herdr records a call when one happens" || bad "22e control" "the recorder never fired, so 22a-22c prove nothing"
  # 22f herdr ABSENT from PATH entirely: no call, no error, rc 0
  ( PATH="$root/emptybin"; HERDR_CALLS="$root/calls.22f"; export HERDR_CALLS; HERDR_ENV=1 HERDR_PANE_ID=w1:p1 label_herdr_pane "x-main" ) >/dev/null 2>&1; rc=$?
  [[ $rc -eq 0 && ! -e "$root/calls.22f" ]] && ok "22f no herdr on PATH -> silent no-op, rc 0" || bad "22f herdr absent" "rc=$rc $( [[ -e $root/calls.22f ]] && cat "$root/calls.22f")"
  print -- "pj selftest: $pass passed, $fail failed"
  [[ $fail -eq 0 ]]
}

# --profile is read BEFORE --dry-run so `pj --profile scratch --dry-run` works, and only
# in leading position so a later --profile passes through to claude untouched.
WANT="${PJ_PROFILE:-default}"
if [[ "${1:-}" == --profile ]]; then
  if [[ -z "${2:-}" ]]; then
    print -u2 -- "pj: REFUSED: --profile needs a name (pj --profile scratch)"
    exit 2
  fi
  WANT="$2"; shift 2   # shift-lint: ok one-shot leading --profile, the -z "${2:-}" refusal above guards it
fi

case "${1:-}" in
  --dry-run)
    shift
    load_profile "$WANT" || exit $?
    build_argv || exit $?
    print -l -- "${PJ_ARGV[@]}" "$@"
    exit 0 ;;
  --dry-run-env)
    # the env half of the dry run, one VAR=value per line. Deliberately a SEPARATE flag:
    # --dry-run must keep printing argv and nothing else, or the byte-for-byte default
    # comparison that hard constraint 1 rests on would compare two different things.
    shift
    load_profile "$WANT" || exit $?
    build_argv || exit $?
    print -l -- "${PJ_ENV[@]}"
    exit 0 ;;
  --selftest) selftest; exit $? ;;
esac

load_profile "$WANT" || exit $?
build_argv || exit $?
if [[ ! -r "$LAUNCH_FILE" ]]; then
  print -u2 -- "pj: REFUSED: ${LAUNCH_FILE/#$HOME/~} missing; stow -R the dotfiles"
  exit 2
fi
kick_dotclaude_fetch
kick_checks_run
# Only on a REAL launch, never on --dry-run or --dry-run-env: a dry run must not touch
# the filesystem, which is the same contract arm 8 holds kick_dotclaude_fetch to.
ensure_registry_link "$P_CONFIG_DIR"
# Same contract: the pane label is a real side effect, so it happens here and not in
# build_argv. The name is read back out of PJ_ENV rather than rebuilt, so the label and the
# session can never disagree -- including when the name came in already chosen.
# PJ_SESSION_NAME is the one to read: it is emitted on EVERY launch, where
# CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_NAME is emitted only when pj computed the name itself.
_pj_name=""
for _pv in "${PJ_ENV[@]}"; do [[ "$_pv" == PJ_SESSION_NAME=* ]] && _pj_name="${_pv#*=}"; done
label_herdr_pane "$_pj_name"
unset _pj_name
source "$LAUNCH_FILE"
# Exported into THIS script's own process, which is safe because the script is its own
# process: nothing leaks back to the interactive shell that typed `pj`. NOT `env VAR=v
# __claude_launch ...` -- `env` execs an external command and __claude_launch is a shell
# function, so that form would fail with "No such file or directory".
# PJ_ENV never contains CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR on the default profile, so the default launch
# still runs with that variable UNSET rather than set to its own default.
for _pv in "${PJ_ENV[@]}"; do export "${_pv%%=*}=${_pv#*=}"; done
unset _pv
__claude_launch "${PJ_ARGV[@]}" "$@"
